note The human is reading the graph and double-clicks a function to see its callers and callees. That serves the goal of browsing the codebase in this tool, from CLAUDE.md. With References closed, a header click only selected the node, and a jump that starts on the Graph tab stays on the graph, so the double-click changed nothing. The user hit that. The test show_references_opens_the_view_after_it_was_closed closes the view and checks that the action brings it back. Opening References by hand and clicking the node again was the only way to the list. The header buttons already reveal callers and callees on the canvas, and they stay the way to do that on the canvas. They do not open the References list after the user has closed it. The source button leaves the graph. Alt-click peeks one identifier. A single click stays select and move, because that gesture already drags the node. A separate button would be another control to find. The default layout already shows References, so leaving it in the layout does not help once the user closes it. Double-click on the node header selects the node and brings References beside the graph, splitting a panel when References was closed. A jump to a definition that starts on the Graph tab does the same, because that double-click was already asking where a call goes, and the list is the answer while the graph stays put.

